([C@H]1NC)O)O[C@H]2[C@@H](C[C@@H]([C@H]([C@@H]2O)O[C@@H]3[C@@H](CC=C(O3)CNCCO)N)N)NC(=O)[C@H](CCN)O)O","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":2018,"bbw":1,"fda":0,"ema":0,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:51:18+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Plazomicin is indicated for the treatment of patients 18 years of age or older with Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (cUTI) including Pyelonephritis, who have limited or no alternative treatment options. It should only be used to treat infections that are proven or strongly suspected to be caused by susceptible microorganisms.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Antibacterial Agents; Anti-Infective Agents; Aminoglycosides","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1392/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Aminoglycosides","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Plazomicin/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Plazomicin exerts its antibacterial activity in a dose-dependent manner with a post-antibiotic effect ranging from 0.2 to 2.6 hours at 2X MIC against _Enterobacteriaceae_, as demonstrated by _in vitro_ studies. In clinical trials comprising of hospitalized adult patients with cUTI (including pyelonephritis), resolution or improvement of clinical cUTI symptoms and a microbiological outcome of eradication were observed at day 5 following the first dose administration of plazomicin. Plazomicin has shown to elicit nephrotoxic, ototoxic, and neuromuscular blocking effects. In clinical trials, it did not induce any clinically relevant QTc-prolonging effects.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Plazomicin exerts a bactericidal action against susceptible bacteria by binding to bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit. Aminoglycosides typically bind to the ribosomal aminoacyl-tRNA site (A-site) and induce a conformational change to further facilitate the binding between the rRNA and the antibiotic. This leads to codon misreading and mistranslation of mRNA during bacterial protein synthesis.   Plazomicin demonstrates potency against _Enterobacteriaceae_, including species with multidrug-resistant phenotypes such as carbapenemase-producing bacteria and isolates with resistance to all other aminoglycosides. Its antibacterial activity is not inhibited by aminoglycoside modifying enzymes (AMEs) produced by bacteria, such as acetyltransferases (AACs), phosphotransferases (APHs), and nucleotidyltransferases (ANTs). Plazomicin was shown to be effective against _Enterobacteriaceae_ in presence of some beta-lactamases. In clinical settings and _in vivo_, bacteria shown to be susceptible toward plazomicin include _Escherichia_ _coli_, _Klebsiella pneumoniae_, _Proteus mirabilis_, and _Enterobacter cloacae_. Other aerobic bacteria that may be affected by plazomicin are _Citrobacter freundii_, _Citrobacter koseri_, _Enterobacter aerogenes_, _Klebsiella oxytoca_, _Morganella morganii_, _Proteus vulgaris_, _Providencia stuartii_, and _Serratia marcescens_.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Administration of 15 mg/kg plazomicin by 30-minute IV infusion resulted in peak plasma concentrations of 73.7 ± 19.7 μg/mL in healthy adult subjects and 51.0 ± 26.7 μg/mL in patients with complicated urinary tract infections (cUTI). The area under the curve (AUC) were 257 ± 67.0 μg.h/mL in healthy adults and 226 ± 113 μg.h/mL in cUTI patients.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Plazomicin predominantly undergoes renal excretion, where 56% of the total administered drug was recovered in the urine within 4 hours following a single 15 mg/kg IV dose of radiolabeled plazomicin in healthy subjects. About less than 0.2% and 89.1% of the total drug were recovered within 168 hours in feces and urine, respectively.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"The mean (±SD) volume of distribution is 17.9 (±4.8) L in healthy adults and 30.8 (±12.1) L in cUTI patients.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Following administration of 15 mg/kg plazomicin by 30-minute IV infusion, the mean (±SD) total body clearance in healthy adults and cUTI patients is 4.5 (±0.9) and 5.1 (±2.01) L/h, respectively.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"The extent of plasma protein binding in humans is approximately 20%. The degree of protein binding was concentration-independent across the range tested in vitro (5 to 100 mcg/mL).","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Plazomicin is not reported to undergo significant metabolism.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"The mean (±SD) half-life of plazomicin was 3.5 h (±0.5) in healthy adults with normal renal function receiving 15 mg/kg plazomicin via intravenous infusion.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12615","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Black Box Warning","items":[{"section":"Black Box Warning","text":"Yes","source":"ChEMBL","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chembl/explore/compound/CHEMBL1650559"}]},{"section":"LiverTox Summary","items":[{"section":"LiverTox Summary","text":"Plazomicin is a parenterally administered, broad spectrum aminoglycoside antibiotic typically used for moderate-to-severe urinary tract infections or pyelonephritis. Plazomicin has had limited clinical use but has not been linked to serum enzyme elevations during therapy or to instances of clinically apparent liver injury.","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Plazomicin/"}]},{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","items":[{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","text":"Intravenous therapy with plazomicin has been linked to only rare instances of serum enzyme elevations ( Likelihood score: E (unlikely cause of clinically apparent liver injury).","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Plazomicin/"}]},{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Plazomicin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic similar to gentamicin and amikacin. No information is available on the use of plazomicin during breastfeeding. However, based on the excretion of other aminoglycoside antibiotics, amounts in milk are expected to be low. Monitor the infant for possible effects on the gastrointestinal flora, such as diarrhea, candidiasis (e.g., thrush, diaper rash) or rarely, blood in the stool indicating possible antibiotic-associated colitis. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1392/"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":1,"forms":[["Concentrate For Solution For I.V Infusion",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":0,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":2018,"bbw":true,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":{"name":"Plazomicin","class":"Aminoglycosides","atc":"J01GB14","category":"Reserve","eml":true}},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:42613186","title":"Plazomicin","products":1,"mw":592.7,"logp":-5.36,"tpsa":269.3,"hbd":11,"hba":15,"rotb":13,"rings":3,"heavy":41,"lipinski":3,"formula":"C25H48N6O10"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:441306","title":"Netilmicin","products":5,"mw":475.6,"logp":-3.2,"tpsa":199.7,"hbd":8,"hba":12,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":33,"lipinski":2,"formula":"C21H41N5O7","similarity":0.605},{"key":"cid:37768","title":"Amikacin","products":64,"mw":585.6,"logp":-8.42,"tpsa":331.9,"hbd":13,"hba":17,"rotb":10,"rings":3,"heavy":40,"lipinski":3,"formula":"C22H43N5O13","similarity":0.426},{"key":"cid:3467","title":"Gentamicin","products":134,"mw":477.6,"logp":-3.33,"tpsa":199.7,"hbd":8,"hba":12,"rotb":7,"rings":3,"heavy":33,"lipinski":2,"formula":"C21H43N5O7","similarity":0.402},{"key":"cid:9855350","title":"Gentamicin Sulfate","products":17,"mw":477.6,"logp"